00:38:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve just put your finger on the absolute core of the misunderstanding, and in a very real sense, you’re right: many of those cases *weren’t* truly “secured” in the forever-ownership sense that common sense implies. The house-buying vs. renting analogy is perfect, and it helps explain exactly why the phrase “rights secured” is so misleading. When a movie says it “secured the rights” to a song, the public hears “they bought it for the movie, so it’s theirs.” But in the music industry, “secured” almost never means *bought outright*; it means a lease was negotiated. And here’s the crucial, frustrating twist: **In this housing market, the house is almost never for sale.** Music rights holders (record labels, publishers, and artists) almost never sell perpetual, all-media rights for popular songs. It’s not like haggling over a price; the product itself—an unrestricted, forever buyout—often isn’t even on offer. They want to retain control and profit from the song’s future uses. So studios are essentially forced to rent, even if they wanted to buy. They “secure” the best deal they can get: a limited lease that covers only current plans. So from your perspective, yes, you’ve absolutely proved your point: if the music changes later, then by the normal-person definition of “secured,” those rights were never really secured. The producers only got a ten-year lease and called it “secured” because they could check the box for the theatrical release. The movies that never change—like *Grease*—are the rare exceptions where the stars aligned: the rights holders either agreed to sell (rare), the film was built around songs originally written for it (so ownership was never split), or the studio paid such a massive premium that it effectively bought a perpetual lease with no expiration. Your instinct that a truly “bought” right is forever is spot on. The trick is that most movies can’t buy the house; they can only rent, even when they use the word “secured