00:39:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is the crux of the whole confusion, and you’ve put it perfectly. It seems impossible: how can a studio sell a movie worldwide on DVD or stream it globally if they only have a temporary, limited lease on the music? The answer lies in the difference between **selling a copy** and **owning the right to keep exploiting that copy forever**. The short version is: They *can* legally sell the thing because the license was valid *at the time of that specific sale and for that specific format*. When you buy a DVD, you’re not buying the future right to have it on a 4K remaster—you’re buying a plastic disc that was manufactured under a license that covered “DVD distribution in North America from 2002 to 2012” (or whatever). The fact that they sold it to you doesn’t give them—or you—the right to keep using the song on new formats or in new territories forever. Here’s how that works in practice, without violating common sense: --- ### 1. The license is often format-specific A studio might secure the rights for: - **Theatrical release worldwide (in perpetuity)** - **VHS and DVD for 10 years in the U.S.** - **Television broadcast for 5 years** When they press the DVDs and ship them to stores, they’re acting under a valid license. Once that license expires, they can’t make *new* DVDs with that song. But the ones already on your shelf? Perfectly legal—they were made when the license was active, so the copy itself is legitimate. That’s why you can still buy a used *WKRP in Cincinnati* DVD set with the original music (if you find it), but the studio can’t press new ones with that same music. --- ### 2. Selling doesn’t require perpetual rights to the music Think of it like this: You can hire a painter to paint your portrait, and the painter gives you a license to hang it in your living room for 10 years. You paid for that right, so you can “sell” that experience (invite people to see it) during those 10 years. After that, you can’t display it anymore—but you’re not a copyright criminal for having hung it for a decade. In film, the “sale” to you (the viewer) is a one-time transaction for a copy that was legally manufactured. The fact that it’s sold in stores or streamed doesn’t mean the studio *owns* the song forever; it means they had a license that allowed them to include it in that specific release. When that release’s license ends, they just stop selling that version. They don’t have to recall your DVD; they simply pull it from stores and streaming and, if they want to keep the movie available, they release a new version with replaced music—under a new, cheaper license. --- ### 3. Streaming is particularly vulnerable Streaming is considered a distinct “format” (like DVD or TV). A song might be cleared for DVD but not for streaming, because in the 1990s, no one contracted for “interactive digital transmission” rights. When a movie moves to Netflix, the lawyers re-ask: “Is this song covered?” If not, they either pay the new fee or swap it. They were never selling the “worldwide perpetual streaming right” when they released the DVD; they were only selling the DVD itself. --- ### 4. Even “worldwide” isn’t the same as “all territory rights in perpetuity” Sometimes a studio buys a perpetual worldwide license for theatrical and home video, but they don’t buy the *music publishing* sync rights for all unidentified future uses. For example, a song might be cleared for “dubbing in any language for theatrical and TV,” but if they later want to include it as a bonus feature in a streaming interface with commentary, that’s a new use. The sale of the original DVD didn’t cover that. --- ### So did they really “secure the rights”? Yes, but only in the way you’d “secure” the right to rent a car for a week. You can drive it, you’re legal, you can even give a friend a lift. But when the rental period ends, you can’t keep driving it. The rental company didn’t sell you the car—they sold you the use of it for a defined period and purpose. Music licensing works the same way. The studio’s press release might say “rights secured,” and for the purposes of that initial release, they were. But they secured a lease, not a deed. Your common-sense assumption that selling a finished product implies permanent ownership is a completely reasonable one—and that’s exactly why these eventual music changes feel so jarring. The music industry’s definition of “secured” is a world apart from the normal person’s.
